                  <provenance type="#m" source="3410"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_295">Lanthony
                     (secunda), Gloucestershire. Augustinian priory of St Mary the Virgin and St
                     John the Baptist</orgName><p>Chapter numbering at foot of pages in pencil and
                     ink in the usual style. See N. R. Ker, in E. G. W. Bill, <title>A Catalogue of
                     Manuscripts in Lambeth Palace Library, MSS. 1222–1860</title> (Oxford 1972), p.
                     11, n. 1: "I did not know of this Lanthony marking in time for the second
                     edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). It numbers books,
                     chapters, psalms, and sometimes leaves, and is always at the foot of the pages
                     in pencil or ink and sometimes in both pencil and ink."</p><p>Cf. M.R. James:
                     "It may be from L." [no. 19 in the Catalogue], but the hand and ornament
                     "strongly suggest" St. Albans..</p><p/><note
                     type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">Cf. cat. 19</note></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#s" cert="low" source="4881"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_474"
                     >St Albans, Hertfordshire. Benedictine abbey of St Alban</orgName><p>This
                     attribution was accepted (albeit with a query) in the second edition of
                     Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). The attribution was later rejected
                     in the Supplement (1987). The attribution was, once again, included with a
                     query in MLGB3 (2010-2015). MLGB3 cited the following evidence, which mirrors
                     the corresponding card in the original Medieval Libraries of Great Britain card
                     index: 'The writing and ornament strongly suggest to me St. Albans as the place
                     of production' (M. R. James).</p></provenance>
               </history>
